#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wf-turn-budget — tell a capped employee it is near its turn limit, once, in time to write.

THE DEFECT, MEASURED. Session `52aabcb1` in odyssey-alive dispatched 18 orders; the 2 that
hit `maxTurns` were both BUILD orders, both had already finished and verified their main
work, and both returned **no report at all**. One was cut at about call 42, during cleanup
and verification. A cut-off is not a failure the orchestrator can read: the run simply
stops, the manager sees nothing, and the finished work is unrecoverable because nobody
wrote down what it was. The user's words, relayed 2026-10-12: *"see if there is anything we
can do to fix this without degrading the current performance … this may be a performance
booster. And by performance, I'm talking about quality of work, not how quick it's done."*

WHY A HOOK AND NOT A SENTENCE IN A HANDBOOK. An agent cannot count its own turns — nothing
in its context says which turn it is on — so a handbook clause asking it to watch the
number is advisory in the shape this project has already measured as not firing (SKILL.md
§ Directives, 2026-08-05: a rule nobody re-reads stops firing). A hook counts. The agent
only has to act on what it is told.

TURNS, NOT CALLS, AND THE DIFFERENCE DECIDES WHETHER THIS IS NOISE. Parallel tool calls
share one turn, so calls >= turns always. MEASURED in the same session: an Invest-watch
report order ran 42 CALLS and the hour-goals producer ran 44, both under a 40-TURN cap,
and both finished. A per-call boundary would have stopped two runs that were fine, on
their 33rd call. The count here is of distinct assistant messages carrying a `tool_use`,
read from the subagent's own transcript.

MEASURED ON CLAUDE CODE 2.1.296, 2026-10-12 (platform.md § MEASURED, fact 22a), and three
of those facts contradict what the design was written from:

  * `agent_id` is present on a PostToolUse payload from inside a subagent and ABSENT on
    the main thread. That is the identity test, and `agent_type` is not: the docs record
    `agent_type` on a `--agent` main thread too.
  * `agent_type` is absent from a `type: "agent"` HOOK's own agent, which still carries
    `agent_id` (`hook-agent-<uuid>`). So both keys are required before anything is read.
  * There is NO `agent_transcript_path` on PostToolUse — that key exists on SubagentStop
    only. `transcript_path` names the PARENT session's transcript. The subagent's own
    transcript sits beside it at
    `<dir of transcript_path>/<session_id>/subagents/agent-<agent_id>.jsonl`,
    every row carrying `isSidechain: true` and `agentId`.
  * `additionalContext` returned from this event DOES reach the subagent: it arrives as a
    `hook_additional_context` attachment in the subagent's transcript, and the canary
    subagent obeyed it on its next call.

CHEAP ENOUGH FOR EVERY TOOL CALL IN EVERY SESSION, which is the licence it has to be in
the default set. `PostToolUse` fires on every call the machine makes, most of them in the
main thread of projects with no org at all. The main-thread branch exits before any
filesystem access whatsoever — one dict lookup. An agent with no handbook costs one
`lstat`, and an uncapped one (every Lead and CEO, which have no `maxTurns`) costs that
plus a 4 KB read. Only a capped IC reaches the counter, and the counter reads the tail of
the transcript from a stored byte offset rather than the file.

THE STATE IS SCRATCH AND LIVES UNDER THE SYSTEM TEMP DIR, NEVER IN THE PROJECT.
`.claude/workforce/work/` is gitignored only by projects whose own audit added the entry,
and nothing workforce ships writes that entry — so a counter file there would show up in
`git status` on every host that installed this and in `git add -A` on some of them. The
state is a cache in both directions: the transcript is the source of truth, and state
that cannot be read is discarded rather than trusted.

NEVER RAISES, EXITS 0 FROM EVERY PATH. A hook that prints a traceback does it in front of
the model on every tool call for the rest of the session, which is worse than the
boundary it was trying to announce.

Exit codes
    0  always, from every path, including a payload it could not parse

Usage
    wf-turn-budget            # a PostToolUse hook; the payload arrives on stdin
"""
import json
import os
import re
import sys


# ── the two thresholds ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WARN is 80% of the cap. It is the one that matters: it arrives with a fifth of the
# budget still unspent, which is enough turns to write an OUTPUT.md and a continuation
# brief and still hand back. For the measured IC cap of 40 that is turn 32.
#
# FINAL is `maxTurns - 3`, and the 3 is the room to obey it. IT WAS `- 2`, AND THE
# END-TO-END RUN OF COMMIT d782174 MEASURED WHY THAT IS ONE TOO FEW: a `maxTurns: 20` IC
# took the final injection on turn 18, spent turn 19 writing `OUTPUT.md` and turn 20
# writing `HANDOFF.md`, and the cap arrived before it could reply — so both files existed
# on disk and the `HANDOFF` verdict never reached the manager, which is the silence this
# hook exists to prevent, one layer in. A written file the manager was never told about is
# not a hand-back. So the budget is three turns: ONE to write both files with parallel
# `Write` calls, which FINAL_TEXT now asks for by name, one of slack for a write that has
# to be retried, and one for the REPLY, which is the only part the manager receives.
#
# EXPRESSED AS A FRACTION OF THE CAP, NOT AS A NUMBER OF TURNS, because the cap is read
# from the handbook and differs per employee. `(n * 4 + 4) // 5` is ceil(0.8n) without
# importing math: 40 -> 32, 80 -> 64, 10 -> 8.
def warn_at(max_turns):
    return (max_turns * 4 + 4) // 5


def final_at(max_turns):
    return max_turns - 3


# ── which allowance a cumulative count lands in ──────────────────────────────
# ONE DEFINITION, READ BY BOTH HOOKS, BECAUSE THE TWO IT REPLACES DISAGREED. This file
# scheduled round k's thresholds at `final_at(cap) + k * cap`, and `wf-widen` reported the
# round as `ceil(turns / cap)`. Both assume a round ENDS on an exact multiple of the cap.
# MEASURED 2026-10-13, session `68b82f98`, cap 10: the harness cut the agent on TURN 8 of
# round 1, not on turn 10. Round 2's final threshold was therefore scheduled for turn 17,
# the second cut arrived first, and THE RESUMED AGENT GOT NO FINAL NOTICE AND WROTE NO
# BRIEF — the silence this hook exists to remove, one round in. Our count and the
# harness's disagree whenever a turn is not a tool call, so with a cap of 80 the same
# drift opens on every resume.
#
# SO A ROUND IS ANCHORED TO WHERE THE PREVIOUS ONE ACTUALLY ENDED, which is a fact the
# agent's own transcript already records: a `SendMessage` resume appends a new PROMPT row
# to it, and the turn count standing at that row is where the next allowance begins
# (`count_turns`). `round_start` is that number, stored beside the count in `count.json`.
# Round k's thresholds are then `round_start + warn_at(cap)` and `round_start +
# final_at(cap)`, so the final notice lands three turns before the limit in EVERY round
# rather than only the first.
#
# THE CAP-MULTIPLE MODEL SURVIVES AS THE BACKSTOP, and it is what makes the anchor safe to
# ship against state written without it. `round_start` is 0 for a run that was never cut,
# and round 1 then behaves exactly as it did; it is also 0 in a counter the previous
# release wrote. When the count has outrun a whole allowance from the anchor, the anchor is
# missing or stale, so the ceiling division answers instead — the number the last release
# printed, for the state it left behind. The direction of the error stays the one this hook
# already chose: an early boundary costs one extra written hand-back, a late one costs the
# run.
def counted(state, key):
    """One stored counter off `count.json`, validated WHERE IT IS READ.

    The counter sits in scratch another account on the host can reach, so a string, a
    float or a negative on any key reads as 0 rather than raising inside a hook — and
    `wf-widen` reads the same file for itself, so the validator is shared rather than
    stated twice with two ideas of what a count is.
    """
    v = state.get(key) if isinstance(state, dict) else None
    return v if isinstance(v, int) and v >= 0 else 0


def turn_round(state, cap):
    """`(turn within this allowance, which allowance, the count the allowance started at)`.

    1-BASED ON BOTH NUMBERS. Allowance 1 is the first one, and the turn inside it is never
    0 after the first call of a round and never past the cap. `wf-widen` prints both in the
    performance record it files, and this hook keys its claim markers on `allowance - 1`,
    so round 0 keeps the marker name it had before any round was counted.
    """
    turns, start, rounds = (counted(state, "turns"), counted(state, "round_start"),
                            counted(state, "round"))
    if not isinstance(cap, int) or cap < 1:
        return turns, 1, 0
    if start > turns:
        start = 0        # state that cannot be true is discarded, as `read_state` does
    spent = turns - start
    if spent > cap:
        extra = (spent + cap - 1) // cap - 1
        rounds += extra
        spent -= extra * cap
        start = turns - spent
    return spent, rounds + 1, start


def keyed(marker, round_):
    """The claim name for one round of `marker`.

    ROUND 0 KEEPS THE BARE NAME, so a marker file written before rounds were counted is
    still read by the release that counts them. One definition because three places build
    this name — the claim, the walk back over the claims, and the test for whether the
    current round's notice has already gone out — and a fourth spelling of it is how a
    resumed agent ends up claiming a marker nobody looks for.
    """
    return marker if round_ <= 0 else "%s-%d" % (marker, round_)


def final_pending(home, state, cap):
    """True when THIS round's warning or final notice has already gone out.

    IT IS WHAT LICENSES A PROMPT ROW TO BE READ AS A NEW ALLOWANCE (`count_turns`'s
    `pending`), so both readers of the transcript have to answer it the same way: the
    `PostToolUse` writer before it folds its tail, and `wf-widen`'s stop before it folds
    the one the writer never got to. A second spelling of the marker name here is how a
    resumed agent's round boundary lands in one hook and not in the other.

    `home` IS THE VERIFIED COUNTER DIRECTORY or "", and "" is False: with no directory
    there is no marker to ask, and the cap-multiple backstop in `turn_round` answers
    instead — the direction this hook has already chosen everywhere else.
    """
    # EITHER NOTICE ENDS A ROUND. The warning itself tells the agent to write HANDOFF.md and
    # return, so an agent that obeys it stops at 80% and never sees the final notice.
    # MEASURED 2026-10-14: a coder handed off at the warning (turn 64 of 80), was resumed,
    # and was cut again at turn 77, because only the final marker licensed the resume as a
    # new allowance and the count ran on from zero.
    if not home:
        return False
    k = turn_round(state, cap)[1] - 1
    return any(os.path.exists(os.path.join(home, keyed(m, k)))
               for m in ("fired-final", "fired-warn", HANDBACK_MARK))


# ── the continuation brief's shape ───────────────────────────────────────────
# THESE SEVEN HEADINGS ARE A CONTRACT WITH `org.md` RUNG 12b, which is the consumer that
# reads the brief and RESUMES THE SAME AGENT from it (e06d730; a fresh agent of the role
# starts the work over, which is the defect K8 removes). Rung 12b verifies "sections 3-7
# filled, the work order in section 1 reproduced verbatim", and before 2026-10-12 that
# pointed at a format defined in no file on disk — `wf-handoff`, the producer, had been
# retired in the simplification release, so `HANDOFF` had a consumer and nothing that
# could satisfy it. `bin/check` requires these exact lines to appear in both files.
BRIEF_SECTIONS = """1. The work order, verbatim — the `REQUEST (verbatim):` block unchanged, never summarised
2. Exit criteria, each MET or UNMET, with its evidence
3. What is done, and the file or command that shows it
4. What is left, as the next concrete action; for a job still running, its log path and the line that means it finished
5. What must not be redone, named file by file
6. The commands to re-run, and what each returned last
7. What went wrong or blocked, and what to try instead"""

# THE INSTRUCTION LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. A second copy in a handbook template or in
# settings.json is the copy that drifts, and it is the one no check on a stranger's
# machine can reach.
WARN_TEXT = """TURN BUDGET — you are at turn {turns} of {cap}, which is 80% of your limit.

The limit is enforced by the harness: when it arrives your run ENDS mid-sentence, your
manager receives no report, and every verified thing you have not written down is lost.
This is the only notice you get, and you cannot count your own turns.

IF YOUR WORK IS ALREADY COMPLETE: finish normally, as your handbook says. Ignore the rest.

IF A JOB YOU STARTED IS STILL RUNNING (a render, a build, anything in the background):
do not wait for it and do not poll it. Hand back now, with its log path and the line that
means it finished in section 4 of the brief. Your manager watches for that line and
resumes you when it appears; nothing else will wake you.

OTHERWISE, START NO NEW WORK and do these three things now, in this order:

1. Write your `OUTPUT.md` at the path your handbook names, with every exit criterion
   marked MET or UNMET and the evidence for each. State UNMET rather than omitting it.
2. Write a continuation brief named `HANDOFF.md` in THE SAME DIRECTORY as that
   `OUTPUT.md` — not at the root of the project — with these seven sections, all filled:
{sections}
3. Return `HANDOFF <path to your OUTPUT.md>` as your verdict, and in that same reply say
   in plain words: RESUME ME with SendMessage — do not start this over with a new agent.

`HANDOFF` ASKS YOUR MANAGER TO RESUME YOU. It hands your work to nobody, and your brief
is not an order for a replacement. Your manager answers it with `SendMessage` to YOU, and
you continue from that brief with a fresh turn allowance and the context you have already
paid for still in place (`org.md` rung 12b). A turn cap is a checkpoint, not an ending. A
fresh employee of your role is spawned only if you report your context exhausted, and the
work starts over when that happens — which is why your reply has to name the resume. What
you cannot do is give yourself more turns, so a brief you do not write is work that
simply stops."""

FINAL_TEXT = """TURN BUDGET — turn {turns} of {cap}. RETURN NOW, with whatever exists.

Three turns left. Write nothing new and verify nothing more.

BOTH FILES IN ONE TURN. Send two `Write` calls in a SINGLE message, in parallel — one
file per turn is how this run ends a sentence short of handing anything back:

* `OUTPUT.md`, at the path your handbook names, each exit criterion MET or UNMET.
* `HANDOFF.md`, in THE SAME DIRECTORY as that `OUTPUT.md` — not at the root of the
  project — with these seven sections, all filled:
{sections}

THEN REPLY `HANDOFF <path to your OUTPUT.md>`, then one line: RESUME ME with SendMessage,
do not start this over with a new agent. Nothing else. That reply is the only part your
manager receives — files you wrote and never named are files nobody reads, and a manager
told only that you stopped spawns somebody else to redo the work you already did. A
partial account replied beats a complete one the limit cuts off."""

# WHAT A DELIVERED INJECTION LOOKS LIKE IN THE TRANSCRIPT, which is how the once-only rule
# survives with no counter directory. DERIVED FROM THE TEXTS rather than restated beside
# them: a second copy of the opening line is a literal that drifts the first time the
# wording is improved, and the symptom would be the paragraph repeating on every call.
WARN_MARK = WARN_TEXT.split("{turns}")[0]
FINAL_MARK = FINAL_TEXT.split("{turns}")[0]

# AND WHAT EITHER OF THEM LOOKS LIKE ON A ROW THAT IS NOT A TURN. A delivered injection
# arrives in the agent's own transcript as a `hook_additional_context` attachment, so the
# row that carries it is neither a turn nor the new prompt that starts a round — it is the
# signal that the round it was sent in is ending. DERIVED FROM THE TWO TEXTS, like the
# marks above: a third copy of the opening line is the one that drifts the first time the
# wording improves.
#
# ASCII ONLY, AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS. The common prefix ends in an em dash, and a
# transcript writer that escapes non-ASCII spells it `\\u2014`, so a needle carrying the
# character itself would match nothing on that host. Everything up to it matches either
# spelling.
#
# AND IT IS NEVER ALLOWED TO BE EMPTY. `b"" in raw` is true of every row, which would read
# every round as ending; a prefix the two texts no longer share would produce exactly that.
# The final notice's own head is the fallback, because that is the injection this exclusion
# exists for.
BUDGET_MARK = (os.path.commonprefix([WARN_MARK, FINAL_MARK])
               or FINAL_MARK).encode("ascii", "ignore").rstrip()

# ── the call that ends a run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A HAND-BACK IS THE LAST CALL OF A RUN, SO A NOTICE ON IT IS READ BY THE NEXT ONE. MEASURED
# 2026-10-30, odyssey-alive session `be539307`, design-evaluator `a4cc76dc67d9761e0`, cap 40:
# run 1 handed back a FAIL report with `SubagentHandback` on its 38th call, and this hook's
# `PostToolUse` for THAT call was the one that crossed `final_at` (count 37, one row behind).
# The final notice landed after the report had already gone. The parent then resumed the
# agent with a NEW order, and the newest budget text in its context was "RETURN NOW … Three
# turns left", from a run that was over. It obeyed: wrote HANDOFF.md on its first call and
# handed back without starting the work. The counter itself had the right answer (round 1
# opened at 38); the agent was reading a notice from the previous run.
#
# SO THIS CALL GETS NO NOTICE AND NO MARK, and the row that carries it is a round boundary
# just like a delivered notice: the harness gives a resumed agent a fresh allowance (that
# agent ran 52 turns against a cap of 40, and `aab6052464b402b32` ran 60), so the next prompt
# row after a hand-back starts one. A message that arrives mid-run, with no hand-back before
# it, still opens nothing; a run the harness CUT has no hand-back row, and its resume is
# licensed by the notice as before.
HANDBACK_TOOL = "SubagentHandback"
HANDBACK_MARK = "handed-back"


# ── a job started in the last fifth of the budget ─────────────────────────────
# MEASURED 2026-10-22, mutze-studio run `org-20261022T115924Z-necklacescale`: an IC took
# the 80% notice on turn 32, launched two renders with `nohup … &` on its NEXT call, and
# handed back. Both renders finished and nothing woke it or its manager; the results sat
# unread for about 20 minutes. The notice had gone out before the job existed, so it could
# not mention it. This one is said on the call that starts the job, once per round, and
# only at or past the 80% mark: earlier, a background job is ordinary work.
#
# IT DETECTS THE LAUNCH, NOT THE JOB. Whether a process is still alive is not something a
# hook can tie to one agent, so the text is conditional and the agent, which knows what it
# started, decides. Its first words are not the budget prefix on purpose: `count_turns`
# reads a row carrying `BUDGET_MARK` as a round ending, and this notice must not move that.
JOB_TEXT = """BACKGROUND JOB, near your turn limit. This call started a job in the background.
Do not wait for it and do not poll it: polling spends the turns you have left. Hand back
now, with the job's log path and the line that means it finished in section 4 of your
HANDOFF.md. Your manager watches for that line and resumes you when it appears; nothing
else will wake you."""
JOB_MARK = JOB_TEXT.split(",")[0]

# A HEREDOC BODY OR A QUOTED STRING IS TEXT, NOT A COMMAND, and the IC in the measured run
# wrote its OUTPUT.md through a heredoc. Both are removed before the `&` test, so a line of
# prose ending in " &" is not read as a launch.
#
# ONE LINEAR PASS, NOT A SUBSTITUTION REGEX. The command is the agent's, and it can carry
# text the agent fetched. A quoted-string pattern with an alternation inside a repeat took
# a MEASURED 73 s on 100 KB of `\"` with no closing quote, in a hook the harness waits on.
# Every character here is visited once, and an unclosed quote or heredoc runs to the end.
HEREDOC_HEAD_RX = re.compile(r"<<-?[ \t]*(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_]\w*)\1")
# A lone `&` ending a command: not `&&`, `|&`, `>&`, `&>` or `\&`, and followed by a space,
# a `)` or the end of a line. `nohup`, `setsid` and `disown` count where a command starts.
BACKGROUND_RX = re.compile(
    r"(?<![&|>\\])&(?=[ \t)]|$)|(?:^|[;&|(])[ \t]*(?:nohup|setsid|disown)\b", re.M)


def code_only(command):
    """`command` with its quoted strings and heredoc bodies removed."""
    out, i, n = [], 0, len(command)
    while i < n:
        c = command[i]
        # `<<<` is a here-string, not a heredoc, so a `<` right after a `<` opens nothing.
        head = (HEREDOC_HEAD_RX.match(command, i)
                if c == "<" and command[i - 1:i] != "<" else None)
        if c == "'":
            j = command.find("'", i + 1)
            i = n if j < 0 else j + 1
            out.append("''")
        elif c == '"':
            j = i + 1
            while j < n and command[j] != '"':
                j += 2 if command[j] == "\\" else 1
            i = j + 1
            out.append("''")
        elif head:
            i = command.find("\n", head.end())
            i = n if i < 0 else i + 1
            while i < n:                      # skip the body, through its closing line
                eol = command.find("\n", i)
                eol = n if eol < 0 else eol
                line, i = command[i:eol], eol + 1
                if line.strip() == head.group(2):
                    break
            out.append("\n")
        else:
            out.append(c)
            i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def launched_background(payload):
    """True when this `Bash` call started a job in the background."""
    if payload.get("tool_name") != "Bash":
        return False
    tool_input = payload.get("tool_input")
    if not isinstance(tool_input, dict):
        return False
    if tool_input.get("run_in_background") is True:
        return True
    command = tool_input.get("command")
    if not isinstance(command, str):
        return False
    return bool(BACKGROUND_RX.search(code_only(command)))


# `agent_type` ARRIVES IN A PAYLOAD AND BECOMES A PATH, so it is validated as a bare
# name and never sanitised into one. Same shape as `wf-org-sync`'s AGENT_SLUG_RX, plus an
# explicit `..` refusal: the leading-character class already blocks a bare `..`, and this
# blocks `a..b` shapes too rather than reasoning about which of them are reachable.
#
# IT REJECTS WHERE `wf-task-tag`'s `session_component` SANITISES, and it is a separate
# function rather than an import of that one. Both halves are deliberate:
#
#   * REJECTING is right here because every name this validates — `agent_id`,
#     `agent_type`, `session_id` — decides whether anything is read or written AT ALL.
#     `agent_type` becomes `.claude/agents/<name>.md`; a name this cannot vouch for means
#     no cap, which means no boundary, which is the silent direction. `wf-task-tag` has to
#     produce a usable filename for a tag it must store, so substitution is right there.
#   * NOT IMPORTING, because loading that sibling to borrow one function costs a MEASURED
#     +35 ms at best and +115 ms under load, per call, on a hook that fires on every tool
#     call of every session. The hook's own total cost is in that range, so the import
#     would more than double it. A local validator defined per script is also the house
#     pattern for exactly this reason — `config_root()` is defined privately in eight
#     other shipped scripts rather than shared.
BARE_NAME_RX = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}$")
MAX_TURNS_RX = re.compile(r"^maxTurns:\s*(\d+)\s*$", re.M)
# The frontmatter is at the head of the file and a handbook is tens of kilobytes. Reading
# 4 KB answers the only question asked of it.
FRONTMATTER_BYTES = 4096


def bare(name):
    """True when `name` is safe to join into a path as one component."""
    return (isinstance(name, str) and ".." not in name
            and bool(BARE_NAME_RX.match(name)))


def config_root():
    """The personal config root, which is where a user-scope agent registration lands."""
    return os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".claude")


def read_cap(root, agent_type):
    """The `maxTurns` in this agent's handbook, or None.

    PROJECT SCOPE FIRST, THEN PERSONAL. An employee is registered at
    `.claude/agents/<name>.md` by `/workforce hire`, and the personal root is where
    `install --personal` puts an `agent` row. A name that resolves in neither has no cap
    and gets no boundary — which is most agents on most hosts, `general-purpose`
    included.
    """
    for base in (os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents"),
                 os.path.join(config_root(), "agents")):
        path = os.path.join(base, agent_type + ".md")
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            continue
        try:
            with open(path, "rb") as fh:
                head = fh.read(FRONTMATTER_BYTES).decode("utf-8", "replace")
        except OSError:
            continue
        if not head.startswith("---"):
            continue
        end = head.find("\n---", 3)
        m = MAX_TURNS_RX.search(head[:end] if end > 0 else head)
        if m:
            try:
                cap = int(m.group(1))
            except ValueError:
                continue
            # A cap of 3 or less puts `cap - 3` at or before turn 0, so the final
            # injection would fire on the very first call and there is nothing useful to
            # say to such a run; a negative one is a typo. The bound moved with the
            # threshold — it was `> 2` when the threshold was `cap - 2`, and it is the
            # same statement: the boundary has to land at least one turn into the run.
            # Left alone rather than clamped.
            if cap > 3:
                return cap
    return None


def agent_transcript(payload, session_id, agent_id):
    """Where this subagent's own transcript sits, derived from the parent's path.

    MEASURED 2026-10-12: PostToolUse carries no `agent_transcript_path` — that key is on
    SubagentStop only — and `transcript_path` names the PARENT session. The subagent's
    file is a sibling directory of it, keyed by session then by agent.
    """
    parent = payload.get("transcript_path")
    if not isinstance(parent, str) or not parent:
        return ""
    return os.path.join(os.path.dirname(parent), session_id, "subagents",
                        "agent-" + agent_id + ".jsonl")


#
# WHAT COUNTS AS WORK THE AGENT PRODUCED, which is the progress signal the stall guard
# needs and the reason this hook counts anything besides turns. MEASURED 2026-10-13
# (session `68b82f98`): an IC wrote eight files in its first round and eight more in its
# second and was flagged stalled at the second cut, because the only progress signal was
# the issue's UNMET set — and the session had recorded no checklist, so both marks carried
# the digest of the empty set. A report is not measurable either: it is named in a
# handbook, this hook is told an `agent_id`, and in that run the report was never written
# at all. What DOES exist at every cut is the agent's own transcript, so the files it wrote
# are counted from the same read that counts its turns. NO SECOND OPEN: a new transcript
# read here would be a second, unguarded door into a path assembled from payload text.
#
# THE WRITE TOOLS BY NAME, and the exact names rather than a pattern. A substring test
# (`"Write" in name`) matches `TodoWrite`, which every agent calls and which produces
# nothing; the opposite shortcut — "anything that is not a read" — counts `Bash`, where
# `ls` and `rm -rf` are the same call. `MultiEdit` is kept although the host no longer
# sends it, because a transcript recorded under an older harness is still read by this one.
#
# A COUNT OF CALLS, NOT OF DISTINCT PATHS, and the direction of the error is the reason.
# This number decides whether an agent is called STALLED and handed to the user instead of
# resumed, so the cheap mistake is to under-count progress: an agent that spent its round
# re-editing three files it had already touched did work, and a distinct-path set would
# call that round empty. One write in the round is progress; no write at all, with the
# session's own UNMET set unmoved, is the stall the guard is for.
WRITE_TOOLS = frozenset(("Write", "Edit", "MultiEdit", "NotebookEdit"))


def count_turns(path, offset, needles=(), pending=False):
    """What the tail of this agent's transcript says, as a dict:

      turns        turns in the rows just read, or None when nothing could be read
      off          the offset to resume from
      seen         how many times each of `needles` appeared
      writes       `WRITE_TOOLS` calls in those rows
      rounds       ROUND BOUNDARIES in those rows — new allowances this agent was granted
      round_start  the turn count standing at the last of them, within this read

    A TURN IS AN ASSISTANT MESSAGE CARRYING A `tool_use`. An assistant row holding only
    `thinking` is not a turn, and a row holding three `tool_use` blocks is one turn, which
    is the whole reason this counts rows instead of calls.

    A ROUND BOUNDARY IS A PROMPT ROW THAT FOLLOWS A DELIVERED NOTICE OR A HAND-BACK, and
    both halves of that are load-bearing. (The hand-back half: `HANDBACK_TOOL`.) A
    `SendMessage` resume appends a user row carrying TEXT, where
    every ordinary user row in a subagent's transcript carries a `tool_result`; that row is
    where the harness's new turn allowance begins, and it is the only thing on this host
    that marks the end of the previous one. `pending` is the second half: a prompt row is
    read as a boundary ONLY when the final notice for the current round has already gone
    out. Nothing else on a transcript can then move the anchor mid-round, and a row of an
    unforeseen shape costs at worst the cap-multiple backstop (`turn_round`) rather than a
    notice postponed into silence. The cost is the same reason: the extra parse is reached
    only after a notice has been delivered, which is once per round, so an ordinary call of
    an ordinary capped agent pays one `in` test for it.

    ONLY WHOLE LINES ARE CONSUMED. The host batches transcript appends on a 100 ms timer,
    so the tail can be a half-written line; the offset advances to the last newline and
    the partial line is re-read next call rather than parsed once and lost.

    `needles` IS HOW THE ONCE-ONLY RULE SURVIVES WITH NO STATE FILE. A delivered injection
    is recorded in the subagent's own transcript as a `hook_additional_context` attachment
    (measured 2026-10-12), so the transcript can answer "did I already say this" when the
    counter directory had to be refused. Only used on that path, where the read starts at
    offset 0 and therefore covers the whole run.

    IT COUNTS THE NEEDLES RATHER THAN NOTING THEM, because a RESUMED agent has to be able
    to be told again (K8): one delivery per round, and the round is what the count is
    compared against. A set could only ever answer "at least once", which would leave a
    resumed run silent at its second cap — the run the user is waiting on.

    `writes` RIDES ALONG ON THE SAME PARSE. It is the number of `WRITE_TOOLS` calls in the
    rows just read, which is the progress signal `agent_handoff` needs and is free here:
    the row is already decoded and its blocks already walked for the turn test.
    """
    # A REGULAR FILE, OR NOTHING. `os.path.isfile` is false for a FIFO, and the open below
    # would otherwise BLOCK on one until a writer appeared — on every tool call, inside a
    # hook the harness is waiting on. The directory part of this path comes from the
    # payload's `transcript_path` and is not validated as a path (only the session and
    # agent components are), so the name is reachable by anything that can set `cwd` or
    # that path. `read_cap` takes the same guard, and it is the one `wf-turn-ledger`'s
    # `_read_lines` has carried since it shipped.
    unread = {"turns": None, "off": offset, "seen": {}, "writes": 0,
              "rounds": 0, "round_start": 0}
    if not path or not os.path.isfile(path):
        return unread
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            fh.seek(offset)
            chunk = fh.read()
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return unread
    seen = dict((n, chunk.count(n.encode("utf-8"))) for n in needles)
    cut = chunk.rfind(b"\n")
    if cut < 0:
        return dict(unread, turns=0, seen=seen)
    turns = writes = rounds = start = 0
    for raw in chunk[:cut + 1].splitlines():
        if b'"tool_use"' in raw:              # cheap reject before any parse
            try:
                row = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if row.get("type") != "assistant":
                continue
            content = (row.get("message") or {}).get("content")
            if not isinstance(content, list):
                continue
            calls = [b for b in content
                     if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_use"]
            if calls:
                turns += 1
                writes += sum(1 for b in calls if b.get("name") in WRITE_TOOLS)
                if any(b.get("name") == HANDBACK_TOOL for b in calls):
                    pending = True            # the run ended here (`HANDBACK_TOOL`)
            continue
        if BUDGET_MARK in raw:
            # THIS HOOK'S OWN INJECTION, arriving back as an attachment. Not a turn, not a
            # prompt, and the one thing on the transcript that says the round is ending —
            # which is what the no-state path has to read it for: there is no marker file
            # there to ask.
            pending = True
            continue
        # A PROMPT ROW IS A USER ROW WITH NO `tool_result` IN IT, tested on the raw bytes
        # so an ordinary call parses nothing extra. The byte test is deliberately the
        # whole of the `tool_result` refusal: a resume message that quotes the words
        # `tool_result` is read as one of them and the backstop answers, where a parse
        # that looked past it could read a tool result as a new allowance.
        if not pending or b'"tool_result"' in raw or b'"user"' not in raw:
            continue
        try:
            row = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
        except ValueError:
            continue
        # A ROW THE HOST ATTACHED TO THE TURN IN PROGRESS IS NOT A PROMPT. Calling `Skill`
        # writes the skill's body as a user text row (MEASURED 2026-10-30, odyssey-alive
        # `a4cc76dc67d9761e0` row 41), and reading an image writes `[Image: original …]` the
        # same way. The host marks both: `sourceToolUseID` names the call that produced the
        # row (all 457 skill loads on this machine carry it, no parent message does), and
        # `turnCompanion` says it belongs to the current turn (the 142 mid-run image rows).
        # Read as prompts, either one opened a round mid-run after a notice.
        if (row.get("type") != "user" or row.get("sourceToolUseID")
                or row.get("turnCompanion")):
            continue
        content = (row.get("message") or {}).get("content")
        if isinstance(content, str):
            text = content.strip()
        elif isinstance(content, list):
            text = "".join(str(b.get("text") or "") for b in content
                           if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text").strip()
        else:
            continue
        if not text or not (offset or turns):
            # THE FIRST PROMPT OF A TRANSCRIPT IS THE ORDER, NOT A RESUME, and a round
            # cannot start on turn 0 because round 1 already did. The test is reached when
            # a notice is pending and the read starts at the top of the file — the state a
            # discarded or squatted counter leaves — and without it that re-read books the
            # work order itself as a new allowance and every later round is off by one.
            continue
        rounds += 1
        start = turns
        pending = False
    return {"turns": turns, "off": offset + cut + 1, "seen": seen, "writes": writes,
            "rounds": rounds, "round_start": start}


def fold_tail(state, tail):
    """Apply one `count_turns` read to the stored counters, in place. Returns `state`.

    TWO HOOKS NOW FOLD THE SAME READ, which is why this is a function and not five lines
    inside `main`. The `PostToolUse` writer folds a tail on every call; `wf-widen`'s
    `SubagentStop` folds one more at the stop, because THE STORED COUNTER IS ALWAYS ONE
    TURN SHORT THERE. MEASURED 2026-10-13, session `83ab658c`: `count.json` read
    `{"turns": 23, "writes": 23}` against a subagent transcript holding 24 tool-call rows
    and 24 `Write` calls, and exactly one unread assistant row sat past the stored `off`.
    The host appends transcript rows on a 100 ms timer and a `Write` returns well inside
    that window, so the row carrying the call this hook was fired FOR is not in the file
    when it reads — the deficit is one at every moment, and the next call's read clears
    it. A stop has no next call. The record filed off that counter said the agent stopped
    at turn 3 of a round it had spent four turns in.

    THE COUNTERS ARE NORMALISED FIRST, through `counted`, because one of the two callers
    reads this state out of a world-writable directory: a string or a negative on `turns`
    must become 0 rather than a `TypeError` inside a hook. After that one pass the
    arithmetic below is plain, which is how the anchor line stays the same text in the one
    place that writes it.

    A TAIL THAT COULD NOT BE READ CHANGES NOTHING HERE, and the caller that has a fallback
    applies its own: `main` books the call count, which errs toward an early hand-off,
    while a stop has no call to book and keeps the number it was given.
    """
    for key in ("turns", "writes", "round", "round_start", "off"):
        state[key] = counted(state, key)
    if tail["turns"] is None:
        return state
    if tail["rounds"]:
        # THE NEW ALLOWANCE STARTS WHERE THE PROMPT ROW STOOD, which is the turn count as
        # of the previous read plus whatever this one counted before it.
        state["round"] += tail["rounds"]
        state["round_start"] = state["turns"] + tail["round_start"]
    state["turns"] += tail["turns"]
    state["writes"] += tail["writes"]
    state["off"] = tail["off"]
    return state


# ── the counter directory, which lives in a place other people can write ─────
# THE WHOLE TEMP-FILE CLASS, AND WHY A FALLBACK IS SAFE. On a shared host the system temp
# dir is world-writable with the sticky bit, so every name under it is a name another
# account can get to first: a directory it owns, a symlink aimed at a file of ours, a
# symlink aimed at a file of theirs. This hook runs on every tool call and must not
# become the lever that writes through any of them. So:
#
#   1. `scratchpad_dir` FIRST, when the payload carries one — the session's own private
#      directory, which is what `wf-task-tag`'s `tag_path` already uses. MEASURED
#      2026-10-12: a `PostToolUse` payload on 2.1.296 does NOT carry that key (the keys
#      are agent_id, agent_type, cwd, duration_ms, effort, hook_event_name,
#      permission_mode, prompt_id, session_id, tool_input, tool_name, tool_response,
#      tool_use_id, transcript_path). It is preferred anyway because other events do
#      carry it and because a host that starts sending it should be used, not ignored.
#   2. Otherwise the temp dir, and every directory level is VERIFIED rather than trusted:
#      not a symlink, a real directory, owned by this uid, and not group- or
#      other-writable. Verified by `lstat` on the handle we are about to use.
#   3. Any refusal returns "" and the caller counts from the transcript with no state at
#      all. It never raises and never falls back to a path it could not verify.
#
# THE NAME IS FIXED RATHER THAN SALTED WITH THE UID, and that is a deliberate trade. A
# hostile local account can squat `<tmp>/wf-turn-budget` and keep the check refusing
# forever — a denial of service against the counter, which degrades to the transcript-only
# path and still produces the right boundary at a little more cost per call. The
# alternative, a uid in the name, is a path no fixture can spell, and this project does not
# ship an untested security control. The attack this refuses is the one that matters:
# writing through somebody else's link.
STATE_MODE = 0o700


def _safe_dir(path):
    """Create or accept `path` as a private directory of ours. True when it is one."""
    import stat as _stat
    try:
        os.mkdir(path, STATE_MODE)
    except OSError:
        pass                                    # already there, or cannot be made
    try:
        # `lstat`, NEVER `stat`: the question is what this NAME is, not what it points at.
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return False
    if not _stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):           # a symlink fails here, as does a file
        return False
    if st.st_mode & 0o022:                      # group- or other-writable
        return False
    getuid = getattr(os, "getuid", None)
    return getuid is None or st.st_uid == getuid()


def state_dir(payload, session_id, agent_id):
    """The verified counter directory for this agent, or "" when none can be had."""
    scratch = payload.get("scratchpad_dir")
    if isinstance(scratch, str) and scratch and os.path.isdir(scratch):
        base = os.path.join(scratch, "wf-turn-budget")
    else:
        import tempfile
        base = os.path.join(tempfile.gettempdir(), "wf-turn-budget")
    path = base
    if not _safe_dir(path):
        return ""
    for part in (session_id, agent_id):
        path = os.path.join(path, part)
        if not _safe_dir(path):
            return ""
    return path


def _open_nofollow(path, flags, mode=0o600):
    """`os.open` with `O_NOFOLLOW` added, so a planted symlink at `path` is refused.

    The flag is absent on some platforms; `getattr` keeps this importable there, and on
    such a platform the directory ownership check above is the control that remains.
    """
    return os.open(path, flags | getattr(os, "O_NOFOLLOW", 0), mode)


# WHAT THE COUNTER HOLDS, DECLARED ONCE AND IN ORDER. The reader, the writer and
# `wf-widen`'s own read of the same file all key on this set, so a key added here reaches
# every one of them — where three literal lists would have left the newest key written by
# one and read by none. A KEY THAT ARRIVES ON STATE THAT NEVER HELD IT READS AS 0, which
# is the honest answer and the compatible one: `writes` was added on 2026-10-13 and
# `round`/`round_start` the day after, and a counter written before either says nothing was
# counted, so nothing is claimed and the first write after the upgrade stores the real
# number. For `round_start` that zero IS round 1, which is the behaviour that shipped.
STATE_KEYS = ("off", "turns", "calls", "writes", "round", "round_start")


def read_state(path):
    """The stored counters, as a dict of `STATE_KEYS` — zeros for anything unreadable.
    Unparsable state is DISCARDED.

    The transcript is the source of truth and this file is a cache, so throwing it away
    costs one full re-read and never a wrong answer. A hook that raised on scratch
    somebody else can write would print a traceback on every tool call afterwards.

    `O_NOFOLLOW`, so a symlink planted at `count.json` is refused rather than read
    through — the read would otherwise be an arbitrary-file read aimed by whoever got to
    the name first.
    """
    zero = dict.fromkeys(STATE_KEYS, 0)
    try:
        fd = _open_nofollow(os.path.join(path, "count.json"), os.O_RDONLY)
    except OSError:
        return zero
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            d = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return zero
    return dict((k, counted(d, k)) for k in STATE_KEYS)


def write_state(path, state):
    """Replace the counter atomically, and never through a link.

    THE TEMPORARY NAME IS CREATED `O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW`, so it cannot be a
    symlink somebody planted and cannot clobber a file that is already there. `os.replace`
    then renames over `count.json`: rename acts on the NAME, so a symlink sitting at that
    name is replaced rather than written through. Those two together are why no file
    outside this directory can be reached by writing here.

    EXACTLY `STATE_KEYS` ARE WRITTEN, validated on the way out as well as on the way in.
    The file is read by two hooks and by anybody's `cat`; a key that only one release ever
    wrote is a key the other reads as 0 and cannot tell from absent.
    """
    tmp = os.path.join(path, "count.json.%d" % os.getpid())
    try:
        fd = _open_nofollow(tmp, os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_WRONLY)
    except OSError:
        return
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            json.dump(dict((k, counted(state, k)) for k in STATE_KEYS), fh)
        os.replace(tmp, os.path.join(path, "count.json"))
    except OSError:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass


def claim(path, marker):
    """True for the ONE caller that creates `marker`. False for every later one.

    `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` RATHER THAN A FLAG IN THE COUNTER, and the difference is a real
    race: parallel tool calls inside one turn fire this hook concurrently, and two
    processes doing read-modify-write on one JSON file can both see the flag unset and
    both inject. Creating a file is atomic, so exactly one of them speaks. `O_NOFOLLOW`
    for the same reason the writer has it — a planted link at this name must be refused,
    not followed, and a refusal here reads as "already claimed", which is the silent
    direction.
    """
    try:
        fd = _open_nofollow(os.path.join(path, marker),
                            os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_WRONLY)
    except OSError:
        return False
    os.close(fd)
    return True


def emit(text):
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"hookSpecificOutput": {
        "hookEventName": "PostToolUse", "additionalContext": text}}) + "\n")


# ── the hand-off mark on the issue ledger (K8) ───────────────────────────────
# THE CUT AGENT'S WORK HAS TO BE FINISHED, NOT MERELY RECORDED. The user, 2026-10-12:
# *"the agents are reaching their turn limits with the task uncompleted … we're waiting on
# work that's never gonna get completed."* The injection above makes the cut READABLE; it
# does not make the session continue the agent. So at the final threshold this hook marks
# the agent `handoff` on its issue, and `wf-widen`'s main `Stop` reads that mark and names
# the one action: resume THIS agent with `SendMessage`. MEASURED the same day: resuming a
# cut coder with `SendMessage` kept its whole context and gave it a fresh turn allowance.
#
# WHY THE HOOK WRITES THE MARK AND NOT `SubagentStop`. This hook knows the `agent_id` and
# the count at the moment the budget runs out. Whether `SubagentStop` fires at all when the
# harness cuts a run at `maxTurns` is UNMEASURED on this host, and a mark that depends on an
# unmeasured event is the silence this whole line of work exists to remove.
#
# WHY THE IMPORT IS IN HERE AND NOT AT THE TOP OF THE FILE. Loading `wf-task-tag` costs a
# MEASURED +35 ms at best and +115 ms under load, and this file is a `PostToolUse` hook on
# every tool call of every session — the reason stated above `BARE_NAME_RX` for not
# importing it to borrow a validator. This path is reached ONCE PER ROUND per capped agent,
# a handful of times in a whole session, so the cost lands where the run is ending anyway.
# The alternative is a second writer of the ledger, which the order forbids and which would
# be the copy that drifts.
def _task_tag():
    """`wf-task-tag` as a module, loaded from beside this file.

    ONE LOADER FOR THE THREE CALLERS — the two ledger writers and the spawn guard's
    reader — because a second copy of these six lines is a second idea of where the
    sibling is and of whether bytecode may be written beside it.
    """
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    # NEVER WRITE BYTECODE: a `__pycache__/` beside a shipped script is an undeclared
    # shipped file, which `bin/check` fails on. `wf-widen`'s `_sibling` carries the
    # same line for the same reason.
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "wf-task-tag")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("wf_task_tag", path)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(
        importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader))
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


def _ledger_write(payload, call):
    """Run one of `wf-task-tag`'s ledger writers and save when it recorded something.

    `call(mod, led)` returns the issue id it landed on, or None for "nothing to record" —
    and None means NOT SAVED, so the ordinary tool call of an already-`running` agent
    rewrites no file.

    Returns that issue id, or None — including when this project has no ledger, which is
    every project that has not run a turn with an issue in it. NEVER RAISES: the boundary
    injection is the part the run cannot do without, and a traceback here would print in
    front of the model instead.
    """
    try:
        mod = _task_tag()
        led = mod.load_ledger(payload)
        issue = call(mod, led)
        if issue:
            mod.save_ledger(payload, led)
        return issue
    except Exception:
        return None


def _ledger_read(payload, call):
    """Run one of `wf-task-tag`'s ledger READERS. Its answer, or None.

    NOTHING IS SAVED HERE and that is the whole difference from `_ledger_write`: the
    spawn guard runs on a `PreToolUse` in the MAIN session, where this hook has never
    written a byte, and a guard that rewrites the ledger it is only consulting would put
    this hook's name on every issue file in every project that has one.
    """
    try:
        mod = _task_tag()
        return call(mod, mod.load_ledger(payload))
    except Exception:
        return None


def mark_handoff(payload, writes):
    """Mark this agent `handoff` on its issue, with the work it produced this round.

    `writes` is its cumulative `WRITE_TOOLS` count; `agent_handoff` compares it against
    the number the previous mark stored and calls the agent stalled only when nothing
    moved on either measure.
    """
    return _ledger_write(payload, lambda mod, led: mod.agent_handoff(payload, led, writes))


# ── and the other end of it: a resumed agent is working again ────────────────
# A `SendMessage` RESUME FIRES NO `SubagentStart`, so nothing else in this distribution
# ever sees it. Without this, a resumed agent's entry sits at `handoff` for the whole
# second round and the main `Stop` of the turn in the middle of it asks the session to
# resume an agent that is already resumed. This hook is the only thing that observes the
# cut agent making a tool call again, so it is the one that can say so.
#
# ONCE PER ROUND, THROUGH THE SAME CLAIM FILE MECHANISM the injections use, and gated on a
# final marker already being claimed — so the cost on every ordinary capped agent is ONE
# `os.path.exists`, and the ledger is loaded only on the first call after a cut.
RESUME_MARK = "resumed-after-"


def last_claim(path, marker, rounds):
    """The newest round-keyed `marker` claimed under `path`, or "" when none was.

    NEWEST FIRST, because the question is "has this agent been cut, and by which claim" —
    and the claim that decides is the last one, so each cut gets exactly one resume flip
    keyed to it. `rounds` bounds the walk, and the caller passes the round BEFORE the one
    it is in: a cut claimed by the current allowance is not a cut this allowance can be
    the resume of. That bound makes it ONE `os.path.exists` for an agent that was never
    cut — almost every agent, on every call — and no walk at all inside a first allowance,
    where `rounds` is -1 and the range is empty.

    KEYED TO THE CUT RATHER THAN TO THE ROUND, and that is measured. In session `68b82f98`
    a `maxTurns: 10` IC was cut at turn 8 and resumed at turn 9, and the cap-multiple
    arithmetic that scheduled the rounds read the same round for both — one round by that
    arithmetic, two allowances in fact. A resume claim keyed by round number would
    therefore have been spent by the cut itself for small caps. The marker that the cut
    actually claimed is the one thing that cannot disagree with how many times this agent
    has been told to hand off.
    """
    for k in range(rounds, -1, -1):
        name = keyed(marker, k)
        if os.path.exists(os.path.join(path, name)):
            return name
    return ""


def mark_running(payload):
    """Put this agent back to `running` on its issue after a resume."""
    return _ledger_write(payload, lambda mod, led: mod.agent_resumed(payload, led))


# ── the wrong action, refused where it is taken (K8, 2026-10-13) ─────────────
# A CUT AGENT IS RESUMED, NOT REPLACED, AND UNTIL NOW NOTHING STOPPED THE REPLACEMENT.
# MEASURED in trial e2e3, session `bb8a3e53`: a cap-10 filer was cut, and in the SAME main
# turn the session spawned a second filer with the `Agent` tool and then a third. The two
# cut agents stayed `handoff` in the ledger for the rest of the session. The kickback that
# names the resume is on the main `Stop`, and a session that never stops between
# dispatches never reaches it — so the correction arrived after all three runs were over.
#
# `PreToolUse` IS THE EVENT, BECAUSE IT IS THE ONLY ONE THAT CAN SAY NO. Every other hook
# in this mechanism reports after the fact. A deny here reaches the model as the reason it
# could not do the thing, in the turn it tried, with the agent_id it should have messaged
# instead — which is the one piece of information a `Stop` three dispatches later cannot
# deliver in time.
#
# THE MAIN SESSION ONLY. `agent_id` is absent on a main-thread payload and present inside
# a subagent (MEASURED 2026-10-12, platform.md fact 22a), and it is the manager that holds
# the resume: an employee spawning a helper of its own is not the measured defect and is
# not refused here.
#
# AND IT NEVER WEDGES A SESSION. Four separate things make the deny lift by itself: the
# issue closing, `agent_resumed` retiring the mark at the resumed agent's next tool call,
# the mark ageing out of `held_by`'s window, and a spawn of any OTHER role, which is never
# touched. A project with no ledger reads `[]` and is never guarded at all.
#
# `{action}` IS NOT SPELLED OUT HERE. It is `wf-task-tag`'s `resume_action`, which
# `wf-widen` reads for the same sentence at the main `Stop` — one mechanism saying one
# thing on two events. Each file used to carry its own wording and the two had already
# drifted by the time they were made one definition (2026-10-13).
DENY_TEXT = """A cut agent of this role is still holding this work, so a fresh one would start it over.

{who} ({atype}) hit its turn cap in this session and is marked `handoff` on this
session's issue ledger — measured 2026-10-12: a coder cut at 80 tool calls was resumed
rather than replaced, and finished at 129.

{action}

`HANDOFF` from an employee is a request to resume THAT agent. It is never a brief for a
replacement. Spawn a fresh {atype} only after the resumed agent reports its context
exhausted, and say so to the user before you do."""


def guard_spawn(payload):
    """Refuse an `Agent` spawn of a role this session has a cut agent of. Always 0.

    SILENT FOR EVERYTHING ELSE, and each exit is one of the conditions the order named:
    another tool, a spawn from inside a subagent, a `subagent_type` that is not a bare
    name, a project with no ledger, a role nobody was cut on, and a mark `held_by` reads
    as stale.
    """
    # TRUTHINESS, NOT `is not None`: the main thread is the payload with NO usable
    # `agent_id`, and an empty string is no more an agent than an absent key is.
    if payload.get("tool_name") != "Agent" or payload.get("agent_id"):
        return 0
    tool_input = payload.get("tool_input")
    want = tool_input.get("subagent_type") if isinstance(tool_input, dict) else None
    if not bare(want):
        return 0
    # EVERY ID IS VALIDATED BEFORE IT BECOMES TEXT IN FRONT OF THE MODEL. These come off
    # a JSON file in the repository, which a person or another tool can edit, and this
    # hook's output is a `permissionDecisionReason` — model context, not a log line. An
    # unvalidated string from that file would be arbitrary text injected into the turn.
    # `bare` is the same refusal `agent_type` takes two lines up.
    #
    # THE MODULE COMES BACK WITH THE IDS, one load for both halves: the ledger answers who
    # is held, and the same module owns the sentence this deny ends on (`resume_action`).
    # Asking for it in a second `_ledger_read` would re-execute the sibling and re-read the
    # ledger to format a string.
    #
    # THE `bare` FILTER STAYS HERE EVEN THOUGH `resume_action` NOW VALIDATES TOO, because
    # the two refusals answer different questions. This one is CONTROL FLOW: a held agent
    # nobody can name leaves `held` empty, and an empty `held` allows the spawn rather than
    # denying it in terms the model cannot act on (`spawn-guard-a-planted-agent-id-is-
    # never-quoted`). The one in `name_agents` only decides what is quoted.
    got = _ledger_read(payload, lambda mod, led: (
        mod, [a for a in (mod.held_by(led, want) or []) if bare(a)]))
    tt, held = got if got else (None, [])
    if not held:
        return 0
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"hookSpecificOutput": {
        "hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
        "permissionDecision": "deny",
        "permissionDecisionReason": DENY_TEXT.format(
            who=", ".join(held), atype=want,
            action=tt.resume_action(held))}}) + "\n")
    return 0


# THE ASKS CHECKLIST, SHOWN TO THE USER WHEN THE MAIN SESSION WRITES IT (`wf-task-tag`'s
# `checklist_display`, which holds the reason and the rules). IT RIDES THIS HOOK because this
# hook already runs on every tool call: a registration of its own on `Write|Edit|Bash` would
# start a second Python process on most calls of every session, and loading `wf-task-tag`
# costs the +35 to +115 ms measured above `_task_tag`. Here a call that does not name the
# checklist costs two string tests on fields already parsed, and only a call that names it
# loads the sibling. `CHECKLIST_PREFIX` there is the literal restated below.
CHECKLIST_PREFIX = "wf-asks."


def checklist_shown(payload):
    """Hand the session the checklist display for this main-thread call when there is one,
    as `PostToolUse` `additionalContext` (`checklist_display` says why not `systemMessage`).
    Checked in the 2.1.312 binary: its `PostToolUse` branch reads
    `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` when `hookEventName` is `PostToolUse`. Always 0."""
    ti = payload.get("tool_input")
    if not isinstance(ti, dict) or not any(
            isinstance(ti.get(k), str) and CHECKLIST_PREFIX in ti[k]
            for k in ("file_path", "command")):
        return 0
    text = _task_tag().checklist_display(payload)
    if text:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"hookSpecificOutput": {
            "hookEventName": "PostToolUse", "additionalContext": text}}) + "\n")
    return 0


def main():
    try:
        payload = json.loads(sys.stdin.read() or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        return 0
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return 0

    # ── THE OTHER EVENT: a spawn about to replace an agent that should be resumed ──
    # Tested first because the fast path below reads the ABSENCE of `agent_id` as "main
    # thread, nothing to do", and the main thread is exactly where this guard runs.
    if payload.get("hook_event_name") == "PreToolUse":
        return guard_spawn(payload)

    # ── THE FAST PATH: no file is touched in the main thread ─────────────────
    # `agent_id` absent means the main thread (MEASURED 2026-10-12), where the only work is
    # the checklist display, and a call that does not name the list touches no file there
    # either (`checklist_shown`). `agent_type` absent
    # with `agent_id` present means a `type: "agent"` hook's own agent, which has no
    # handbook and no cap — also measured, and not predicted by the design.
    agent_id = payload.get("agent_id")
    agent_type = payload.get("agent_type")
    if agent_id is None:
        return checklist_shown(payload)
    if not bare(agent_id) or not bare(agent_type):
        return 0

    root = payload.get("cwd")
    if not isinstance(root, str) or not os.path.isdir(root):
        root = os.getcwd()
    cap = read_cap(root, agent_type)
    if cap is None:
        return 0

    session_id = payload.get("session_id")
    if not bare(session_id):
        session_id = "no-session"
    tpath = agent_transcript(payload, session_id, agent_id)
    path = state_dir(payload, session_id, agent_id)

    if path:
        state = read_state(path)
        # HAS THIS ROUND'S NOTICE ALREADY GONE OUT? `count_turns` needs the answer before
        # it reads, because that is what licenses it to read a prompt row in the tail as
        # the start of a new allowance. One `os.path.exists` on the ordinary call of an
        # agent that has never been cut, which is almost every call.
        pending = final_pending(path, state, cap)
        state["calls"] += 1
        tail = count_turns(tpath, state["off"], pending=pending)
        if tail["turns"] is None:
            # THE FALLBACK ERRS TOWARD AN EARLY HANDOFF. Calls >= turns always, so a call
            # count crosses the boundary no later than the true turn count would. An early
            # written hand-back costs one extra order; a silent cut-off costs the run.
            state["turns"] = state["calls"]
        else:
            fold_tail(state, tail)
        write_state(path, state)
        spent = {}
    else:
        # NO DIRECTORY COULD BE VERIFIED, SO THERE IS NO STATE AND THE RUN STILL GETS ITS
        # BOUNDARY. The count is taken from the whole transcript rather than a tail, and
        # the once-only rule is answered by the transcript too: a delivered injection is
        # recorded there as a `hook_additional_context` attachment (measured 2026-10-12),
        # so what was already said can be read back. The rounds come off the same read,
        # which is why `pending` defaults to False here: the whole run is in front of it,
        # injections included, so it can see for itself which prompt row ended a round. No
        # transcript AND no state leaves nothing to count, and a number nothing measured is
        # not worth stopping a run on.
        tail = count_turns(tpath, 0, (WARN_MARK, FINAL_MARK, JOB_MARK))
        if tail["turns"] is None:
            return 0
        spent = tail["seen"]
        state = {"turns": tail["turns"], "writes": tail["writes"],
                 "round": tail["rounds"], "round_start": tail["round_start"]}
    writes = state["writes"]
    # THE ROUND, ANCHORED TO WHERE THE LAST ONE ENDED. One function answers it for this
    # hook and for `wf-widen`'s record of the same stop, so the schedule and the line that
    # reports it cannot drift apart again — which they had, and the cost was a resumed
    # agent that reached its second cap in silence.
    in_round, allowance, _start = turn_round(state, cap)
    round_ = allowance - 1

    def once(marker, mark, round_=0):
        """True for the one call that gets to say this, ONCE PER ROUND.

        A ROUND IS ONE TURN ALLOWANCE, AND A RESUMED AGENT IS ON ITS SECOND. The counter
        reads one transcript that keeps growing across a `SendMessage` resume, so `turns`
        never goes back to zero — and the marker file lives under the same session and
        agent, so a round-blind claim is spent forever by the first cut. A resumed agent
        would then hit its new cap with no notice at all, which is the defect this hook
        exists for, one layer out. The marker is therefore keyed by round; round 0 keeps
        today's name, so a claim written before this change is still read.
        """
        if path:
            return claim(path, keyed(marker, round_))
        return spent.get(mark, 0) <= round_

    # BOTH DUE AT ONCE MEANS THE STRONGER ONE SPEAKS, and under `cap - 3` the final one is
    # due FIRST for every cap below 15 (`maxTurns: 10` puts the final at 7 and the 80%
    # warn at 8). That is handled rather than special-cased: the final branch is tested
    # first and spends the warn unspoken, and the final text carries the whole instruction
    # — both files, their shape, and the verdict — so an agent that only ever sees this one
    # is told everything the warn would have told it. Emitting two paragraphs on one call
    # would spend the budget it is trying to protect.
    # BOTH THRESHOLDS ARE READ INSIDE THE ALLOWANCE, which is the whole of the fix: the
    # turn compared against them is `turns - round_start`, so round 2 of a cap of 10 fires
    # its final notice at the anchor plus 7 whatever the anchor is — turn 15 for a round
    # that ended at 8, and turn 157 for one that ended on the nose at 80.
    cut_now = False
    if payload.get("tool_name") == HANDBACK_TOOL:
        # THE RUN IS OVER, so nothing is said into it and nothing is claimed: a notice here
        # is read by the NEXT run as its own (`HANDBACK_TOOL`), and a `handoff` mark here
        # would hold the role against an agent that just delivered. The round this call
        # ends is closed by the hand-back row itself, in `count_turns` — and by this claim
        # when that row is already in the file this call read, since the read that consumed
        # it stores no `pending` for the prompt row that comes after (`final_pending`).
        if path:
            claim(path, keyed(HANDBACK_MARK, round_))
    elif in_round >= final_at(cap):
        once("fired-warn", WARN_MARK, round_)      # spend the quieter one unspoken
        if once("fired-final", FINAL_MARK, round_):
            # THE MARK BEFORE THE TEXT. The injection is what the agent acts on, and the
            # mark is what the session acts on; if only one of them can land, the one that
            # survives a crash in the other is the one the user is waiting on.
            cut_now = True
            mark_handoff(payload, writes)
            emit(FINAL_TEXT.format(turns=in_round, cap=cap, sections=BRIEF_SECTIONS))
    elif in_round >= warn_at(cap) and once("fired-warn", WARN_MARK, round_):
        emit(WARN_TEXT.format(turns=in_round, cap=cap, sections=BRIEF_SECTIONS))
    # A JOB STARTED AFTER THE NOTICE, which the notice could not name (`JOB_TEXT`). Never on
    # a call that already spoke, and never past the final threshold, whose branch above
    # owns the call: one paragraph per call, and the final notice already says return now.
    elif (in_round >= warn_at(cap) and launched_background(payload)
          and once("fired-job", JOB_MARK, round_)):
        emit(JOB_TEXT)
    # AND THE OTHER END: THIS CALL IS A CUT AGENT WORKING AGAIN. Not on the call that just
    # told it to resume, which would flip the mark it had this moment written. No state
    # directory means no claim and no flip: the transcript records an injection but not a
    # resume, so such an agent keeps its `handoff` mark until it delivers — one resume asked
    # for twice, which is the recoverable direction against a hold that never ends.
    #
    # A LATER ROUND, NEVER A LATER CALL, AND THE EVIDENCE IS TRIAL e2e3. The notice fires
    # at `cap - 3`, which leaves the agent THREE MORE TURNS in the same allowance — so a
    # bound of `round_` found the cut this round had just claimed and read the agent's own
    # next tool call as a resume. Filer `a26b1c41eecfb3119` was flipped back to `running`
    # that way after each of its two notices, the harness cut it for real at the end, and
    # `agent_returned` recorded `returned` on an agent that was holding work. A resume is
    # a NEW ALLOWANCE: `turn_round` only reports one after `count_turns` saw the prompt row
    # that opened it, so the cut being answered has to belong to an EARLIER round than the
    # one this call is in. `round_ - 1` is that test, and at `round_ == 0` the walk is
    # empty and no agent still inside its first allowance is ever called resumed.
    if path and not cut_now:
        cut = last_claim(path, "fired-final", round_ - 1)
        if cut and claim(path, RESUME_MARK + cut):
            mark_running(payload)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    # NEVER RAISES. Anything unforeseen is silence, because the alternative is a
    # traceback in front of the model on every tool call for the rest of the session.
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except Exception:
        sys.exit(0)
